    "content": "When Korn released *Korn III: Remember Who You Are* on July 13, 2010, it answered the question the nu-metal world had been asking since founding drummer David Silveria's 2006 departure: could anyone fill that seat without losing Korn's identity? Ray Luzier's answer was emphatic. Debuting at #7 on the US Billboard 200 — Korn's highest chart position in years — the album proved the band could not only survive the transition but thrive with a new rhythmic foundation.\n\nRay Luzier (born February 11, 1970, in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania) arrived in Korn as one of the most experienced professional drummers in rock. His years as David Lee Roth's touring drummer and his stint with the Army of Anyone supergroup had built him into a session-caliber player who could lock in with any musical context. But Korn presented a specific challenge: a band with a deeply idiosyncratic groove identity, built around the locked interaction between Silveria's drumming and bassist Fieldy's percussive, syncopated low-end.\n\nLuzier cleared the bar decisively. Producer Ross Robinson — the same producer who had helmed Korn's original debut in 1994 and Slipknot's landmark recordings — returned for *Korn III* with the explicit mission of stripping the band back to its raw, early-era roots. The album is aggressive, dense, and driven by the Luzier/Fieldy rhythm section's ability to anchor songs like \"Oildale (Leave Me Alone),\" \"Let the Guilt Go,\" and \"Pop a Pill\" with the kind of dark, hypnotic groove that Korn had built their identity on.\n\nFor the *Korn III* sessions, Luzier was running a DW Collector's Series setup — DW's professional shell line, built for the demands of studio recording and world touring. This article breaks down every element of that rig: the DW Collector's drum kit, DW Collector's snare, Paiste 2002 and Meinl Byzance cymbals, and DW 9002 double bass pedal that powered one of nu-metal's most celebrated comeback records.",
